\chapter{E-Graphs and Equality Saturation}
\label{ch:egraphs}

The rewrite engine of \chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} is a bottom-up tree
rewriter. It walks a tree, replaces subtrees that match a rule, and produces
a new tree. That model is wonderfully simple, and it is exactly right for
the many uses where a term has one meaning and the rules are chosen so that
rewriting is confluent. It is exactly wrong for the other uses, the ones
where a term has several equivalent readings and the rules do not
converge on their own.

Consider the two laws
\begin{quote}
\code{(add ?x 0) = ?x} \qquad and \qquad \code{(mul ?x 1) = ?x}.
\end{quote}
Applied to \code{(mul (add a 0) 1)} they give two different answers. A
bottom-up engine that rewrites the innermost match first produces
\code{(mul a 1)}, which then reduces to \code{a}. A bottom-up engine that
rewrites the outermost match first produces \code{(add a 0)}, which then
reduces to \code{a}. Both end at \code{a}, but only because these two
particular laws happen to agree; add a law such as
\code{(add ?x ?y) = (add ?y ?x)} and the orders begin to disagree, and the
engine has thrown away the information that would have let it discover
that.

Equality saturation takes the opposite approach. Instead of computing a
successor tree, it computes the set of terms that are \emph{equal} to the
input under the rewrite rules, and it keeps expanding that set until no
rule can fire. The result is a graph rather than a tree: the nodes are
terms, and the edges record which nodes are the same. Because the graph
retains every alternative that was ever derived, an ordering question like
the one above never arises---there is no ordering, because nothing is
overwritten. Only at the very end, when a single concrete term is
required, does a choice get made, and it is made with full knowledge of
every alternative.

DSLtk's \api{dsl::EGraph} is a compact but complete implementation of this
idea: hash-consed e-nodes, union-find e-classes, a congruence rebuild, a
bounded saturation loop, and a minimum-cost extraction. This chapter
describes each of those parts, in the order the implementation presents
them.

\section{The data model}

The public vocabulary of the e-graph is five small types and one class.
\code{EClassId} is a \code{std::size_t} (\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, line 9358) and
is the only handle a user ever holds on the graph. \code{ESubstitution} is
an \code{std::unordered_map<std::string, EClassId>} that maps variable
names, complete with their leading question mark, to the classes they were
bound to. \code{EMatch} pairs the matched class with the substitution that
produced the match, and \code{ERewriteRule} is the triple of a name, a
left-hand side, and a right-hand side. The two remaining types describe
the outcome of a saturation run. The listing gathers declarations that
the header spreads over several hundred lines; it is a transcription, not
a contiguous excerpt.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The public value types of the e-graph layer, gathered into one place.},label=lst:egraph-types]
using EClassId = std::size_t;
using ESubstitution = std::unordered_map<std::string, EClassId>;

struct EMatch
{
  EClassId root;
  ESubstitution bindings;
};

struct ERewriteRule
{
  std::string name;
  SExpr lhs, rhs;
};

struct SaturationLimits
{
  std::size_t iterations = 30, nodes = 100000, matches = 100000;
};

enum class SaturationStop
{
  Saturated,
  IterationLimit,
  NodeLimit,
  MatchLimit
};

struct SaturationResult
{
  SaturationStop stop = SaturationStop::IterationLimit;
  std::size_t iterations = 0, applications = 0;
  bool
  saturated () const
  {
    return stop == SaturationStop::Saturated;
  }
};
\end{lstlisting}

The three limits have distinct meanings, and confusing them is the most
common source of surprise. \code{iterations} bounds the number of passes
over the rule list. \code{nodes} is a budget on the number of distinct
e-nodes the graph may retain; it is installed for the duration of the
saturation and removed afterwards. \code{matches} bounds the total number
of rule applications. A run that stops because of any of the three is
\emph{not} saturated, and \code{SaturationResult::saturated} returns true
only for the \code{Saturated} stop reason. The distinction matters because
a partially saturated graph is a perfectly usable object---it contains
every equality that was discovered before the budget ran out---but it is
not a proof that no further equality exists.

\section{Internal representation}

The graph is stored in three pieces. \code{classes_} is a vector of
\code{EClass} records, each of which holds a union-find parent and a list
of e-nodes. \code{hash_} maps a canonical string encoding of an e-node
to the class that contains it. \code{revision_} is a monotonic counter
that is bumped whenever the graph changes in a way that might enable a new
match.

\subsection{Hash-consing}

An \code{ENode} is a small record: an operator string, a flag saying
whether the node is an atom, a flag saying whether the atom was quoted,
and a vector of child class identifiers. Two e-nodes are the same node if
and only if their canonical encodings agree, and the canonical encoding is
a string built by the private \code{key} function (\texttt{DSLtk.hpp},
line 9481):

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption=The canonical key of an e-node.,label=lst:egraph-key]
std::string
key (ENode &node)
{
  std::string out = std::string (node.atom ? "a" : "n")
                    + (node.quoted ? "q" : "u")
                    + std::to_string (node.op.size ()) + ":" + node.op;
  for (auto &child : node.children)
    {
      child = find (child);
      out += ":" + std::to_string (child);
    }
  return out;
}
\end{lstlisting}

Three details are worth extracting. The one-letter prefixes distinguish an
atom from a list and a quoted atom from an unquoted one, so \code{(a)} and
\code{"a"} are different nodes. The length prefix on the operator makes
the encoding injective, so an operator \code{ab} with no children cannot
collide with an operator \code{a} followed by a child. And the children are
\emph{canonicalised} by \code{find} before they are encoded, which is what
makes the key a function of the equivalence class rather than of a
particular identifier. Hash-consing then means that inserting a node whose
key is already present does not create a new class at all; it returns the
class that already holds that node.

The consequence is that a class may contain several e-nodes. Those are
the \emph{alternatives}: different spellings of the same value. The
atom \code{a} and the list \code{(f a)} may end up in the same class when
a rule equates them, and both remain in the class, so a later match may
use either.

\subsection{Union-find}

The classes are organised by a union-find with path compression.
\code{find (id)} walks the parent chain to a root and then rewrites the
chain so that every node on it points directly at the root
(\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, line 9582). \code{unite (a, b)} merges two roots; if
they differ, the \emph{smaller} identifier wins and becomes the root, and
the losing class's e-nodes are moved into the winner.

The smaller-id-wins rule is not an optimisation but a design choice. It
makes the representative of a class a deterministic function of the
membership of the class, so two runs that perform the same unions in
different orders agree on the identifiers they hand back. A user who
stores an \code{EClassId} across a saturation run can rely on it still
denoting the same value afterwards: identifiers are never reused and
never invalidated, they simply acquire a different representative.

\subsection{Congruence and rebuilding}

The subtle part of an e-graph is congruence: if two nodes have equivalent
children, they are themselves equivalent. The graph cannot be correct
merely by merging classes explicitly, because a union can make two
previously distinct nodes equal without anyone noticing.

The private \code{rebuild} function (\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, line 9616)
implements the congruence closure. It is called lazily: \code{unite} sets
a \code{dirty_} flag, and the next operation that needs a consistent view
calls \code{rebuild} first. The function collects every e-node together
with its owner class, then repeatedly clears the hash table, re-encodes
every node with canonical child identifiers, and merges the owner of any
node whose key is already present. It repeats until a pass makes no
merge, which is the fixed point: once no two distinct nodes have the same
key, the table is a correct congruence-closed index. Finally it clears the
per-class node lists and refills them from the surviving distinct nodes,
so that each class holds exactly the alternatives that are still
distinct.

The fixed point is necessary rather than merely tidy. Merging two classes
can make a third pair of nodes equal, which can make a fourth pair equal,
and so on; a single pass is not enough in general.

\begin{pseudocode}[Rebuild: enforce congruence]
\textbf{function} rebuild (graph)\\
\quad if the graph is not dirty then return\\
\quad collect every e-node together with its owning class\\
\quad repeat\\
\qquad\qquad clear the hash table\\
\qquad\qquad for each collected (owner, node) do\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad encode the node with canonical child identifiers\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad if the encoding is already in the table then\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad merge the owner with the class in the table\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad else\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad insert the encoding, pointing at the owner's root\\
\qquad until a whole pass performs no merge\\
\quad clear the per-class node lists\\
\quad for each collected (owner, node) do\\
\qquad\qquad encode the node again\\
\qquad\qquad if the encoding is new then append the node to its root class\\
\quad clear the dirty flag
\end{pseudocode}

