\subsection{Adding terms}

The three \code{add} overloads all funnel into the same implementation.
\code{add (std::string_view)} parses the text with \code{parse_sexpr}
and forwards; \code{add (const ASTNode &)} converts the tree with
\code{ast_to_sexpr} and forwards; \code{add (const SExpr &)} validates
the term and then instantiates it, creating an e-node for every atom and
every list and returning the class of the outermost node.

The validation is a genuine restriction and not a formality. The
validator of \chapref{ch:sexpressions} collects the pattern variables of
the term, and \code{add} refuses any term that has one. The exception is
a \code{std::invalid_argument}, and the message is \emph{cannot add an
unbound pattern to an e-graph}. An e-graph holds \emph{values}; a pattern
variable is a hole in a pattern, and a hole has no value to occupy. The
same validator rejects a list whose head is not a literal atom, because a
node must have an operator name.

\subsection{Matching}

\code{matches} takes a pattern and returns the list of ways it occurs in
the graph. The implementation walks the \emph{root classes only}
(\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, line 9704): a non-root class is by definition the
same class as its root, so scanning it would produce duplicate matches.
Each match is an \code{EMatch} whose \code{root} is the class the pattern
matched and whose \code{bindings} maps the pattern's variables to the
classes they were bound to.

Matching is a recursive descent over the pattern, and it has two
properties worth spelling out. A pattern variable matches any class, but
if the variable has already been bound in this partial match, the class it
is bound to must be the same class; that is how a repeated variable
enforces equality of two subterms. And a list pattern matches an e-node
only when the node's operator and quoted flag agree with the pattern head
\emph{and} the node has exactly as many children as the pattern has
arguments. The arity check is exact, not a minimum: there is no notion of
a variadic operator.

The \code{limit} argument bounds the number of matches returned, and it
defaults to 100000. A limit of zero returns an empty vector.

\subsection{Observing equality}

\code{when_equal (a, b, fn)} is a conditional observer. It first asks
whether the two classes are already equivalent; if they are not, it
returns \code{false} and does nothing. If they are, it invokes the
callback with the graph and the canonical root identifier and returns
\code{true}. The callback receives \code{(EGraph &, EClassId)}, so it can
extract a representative, add new terms, or record what it saw.

The word \emph{conditional} is the important part. The callback fires
only on equality that already holds; it is not a hook that fires when
equality is later established. A user who wants to be notified of a
discovered equality during saturation should use the \code{on_match}
observer of \code{saturate} instead, which fires after each rule
application.

\section{Saturation}

\code{saturate} is the engine. It takes a vector of rules, a set of
limits, and an optional observer, and it runs the rules against the graph
until nothing more can be derived or a budget is exhausted
(\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, line 9726).

The function begins by re-validating every rule, including rules that were
built by hand rather than parsed. It does this by synthesising the
S-expression \code{(rewrite name lhs rhs)} for each rule and passing it
through \code{sexpr_rule}, so a hand-built rule with an unbound
right-hand variable or a non-literal operator head is rejected with
exactly the same diagnostics as a parsed one. Validation happens before
anything else, so a malformed rule never leaves a half-modified graph
behind.

It then installs the node budget for the duration of the run, using a
small scope guard that restores the previous budget when the function
returns, so a saturation never leaves the graph permanently unable to
grow. Before the loop begins it checks the current node count against the
budget; a graph that is already over budget stops immediately with
\code{NodeLimit} and zero iterations.

The loop itself runs at most \code{limits.iterations} passes. Each pass
rebuilds the graph, snapshots \code{revision_}, and then walks the rule
list in order. For each rule the graph is asked for the matches of the
rule's left-hand side, and for each match the right-hand side is
instantiated against the match's bindings and united with the matched
class. Each such application increments the application's counter and
fires the observer. At the end of the pass the graph is rebuilt again and
the revision counter is compared with the snapshot: if the two are equal,
nothing changed during the pass, no rule can fire again, and the run stops
with \code{Saturated}.

\begin{pseudocode}[Saturate to a fixed point]
\textbf{function} saturate (graph, rules, limits, observer)\\
\quad validate every rule\\
\quad install the node budget for the duration of the run\\
\quad if the node count exceeds the budget then\\
\qquad return a report stopping with \emph{NodeLimit}\\
\quad for each pass from one to the iteration limit do\\
\qquad\qquad rebuild the graph\\
\qquad\qquad snapshot the revision counter\\
\qquad\qquad for each rule do\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad find the matches of the rule's left-hand side\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad for each match do\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad if the match budget is exhausted then\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad return a report stopping with \emph{MatchLimit}\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad instantiate the right-hand side\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad merge it with the matched class\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad count the application and notify the observer\\
\qquad\qquad rebuild the graph\\
\qquad\qquad if the revision counter is unchanged then\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad return a report stopping with \emph{Saturated}\\
\quad return a report stopping with \emph{IterationLimit}
\end{pseudocode}

The revision counter is the criterion for saturation, and it is worth
being precise about what it counts. It is bumped when a node is inserted
and when two classes are merged. It is \emph{not} bumped when an
application leaves the graph unchanged, which happens whenever a rule
fires on a class that is already equal to its right-hand side. A rule
whose left-hand side is a bare variable, for instance, matches every root
class and produces a fresh application each time even though no union
occurs. The application counter therefore grows while the revision counter
stays still, and it is the revision counter that decides that the run has
reached its fixed point.

The observer receives \code{(const ERewriteRule &, const EMatch &)} and
is called after the merge, so the graph it sees already contains the
newly established equality.

