\section{Extraction}

Saturation leaves the graph holding a set of equivalent terms, but a
concrete program usually wants one term. \code{extract} computes the
cheapest finite representative of a class, where the cost of a term is its
node count.

The computation is a fixed point over the class graph
(\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, line 9795). Every class starts with an infinite cost.
The cost of an e-node is one plus the sum of the costs of its children,
and a node whose child has an infinite cost has an infinite cost itself.
A class's cost is the minimum over its alternatives. Because a class's
cost depends on the costs of strictly smaller classes in the acyclic case,
and because the loop repeats until nothing changes, the iteration
converges. The infinity guard is what makes the cyclic case safe: a class
that contains only cyclic alternatives never acquires a finite cost.

When the computation finishes, the chosen e-node for each class is
recorded, and a second pass rebuilds the tree from those choices. The
reconstruction follows child class identifiers, so it is well defined
precisely because the chosen alternatives are acyclic.

If the requested class has no finite representative at all, \code{extract}
throws \code{std::runtime_error} whose message is \emph{e-class has no
finite expression}. The guard turns what would otherwise be an infinite
reconstruction into a diagnosable error.

It is worth being precise about how reachable that guard is, because the
answer is not obvious. The public API cannot normally produce such a
class. Every class is created by \code{insert} from a node whose children
already exist, and every merge only ever adds nodes to a class, so each
class permanently retains at least one e-node whose children terminate in
an atom class; an atom costs one, and the cost therefore always has a
finite value. The guard is best read as a defensive assertion about the
shape of the graph rather than as an error a well-behaved program will
ever see.

\code{extract_ast} is a one-liner on top of \code{extract}. It converts
the extracted S-expression with the bridge of
\chapref{ch:sexpressions}, and therefore inherits the
leaf-versus-inner-node convention described there.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption=Saturating an arithmetic e-graph and extracting the minimum.,label=lst:egraph-saturate]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

namespace
{
const char *
stop_name (dsl::SaturationStop stop)
{
  switch (stop)
    {
    case dsl::SaturationStop::Saturated:
      return "Saturated";
    case dsl::SaturationStop::IterationLimit:
      return "IterationLimit";
    case dsl::SaturationStop::NodeLimit:
      return "NodeLimit";
    case dsl::SaturationStop::MatchLimit:
      return "MatchLimit";
    }
  return "?";
}
}

int
main ()
{
  using dsl::EGraph;

  const std::string ruleText = R"(
(rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)
(rewrite mul-one  (mul ?x 1)  ?x)
)";

  auto rules = dsl::sexpr_rules (ruleText);
  std::cout << "rules parsed: " << rules.size () << "\n";

  EGraph graph;
  auto root = graph.add ("(mul (add a 0) 1)");
  std::cout << "before: classes=" << graph.class_count ()
            << " nodes=" << graph.node_count () << "\n";

  int fired = 0;
  auto report = graph.saturate (
      rules, dsl::SaturationLimits{},
      [&] (const dsl::ERewriteRule &rule, const dsl::EMatch &match) {
        ++fired;
        std::cout << "  applied " << rule.name << " at class " << match.root
                  << "\n";
      });

  std::cout << "stop=" << stop_name (report.stop)
            << " saturated=" << std::boolalpha << report.saturated ()
            << " iterations=" << report.iterations
            << " applications=" << report.applications << " observed=" << fired
            << "\n";
  std::cout << "after:  classes=" << graph.class_count ()
            << " nodes=" << graph.node_count () << "\n";
  std::cout << "minimum: " << graph.extract (root).dump () << "\n";
  std::cout << "as AST : " << graph.extract_ast (root).dump () << "\n";

  auto atom = graph.add ("a");
  bool equal = graph.when_equal (
      root, atom, [] (EGraph &g, dsl::EClassId id) {
        std::cout << "  observer saw canonical class " << id << " -> "
                  << g.extract (id).dump () << "\n";
      });
  std::cout << "when_equal fired: " << equal << "\n";

  EGraph bounded;
  bounded.add ("(add a 0)");
  auto limited = bounded.saturate (rules, dsl::SaturationLimits{ 1, 100000, 100000 });
  std::cout << "one-iteration run stops at " << stop_name (limited.stop)
            << " after " << limited.iterations << " iteration(s), "
            << limited.applications << " application(s)\n";
  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output of \lstref{lst:egraph-saturate} is:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption=Output of \lstref{lst:egraph-saturate}.]
rules parsed: 2
before: classes=5 nodes=5
  applied add-zero at class 2
  applied mul-one at class 4
  applied add-zero at class 0
  applied mul-one at class 0
stop=Saturated saturated=true iterations=2 applications=4 observed=4
after:  classes=3 nodes=5
minimum: a
as AST : (atom a)
  observer saw canonical class 0 -> a
when_equal fired: true
one-iteration run stops at IterationLimit after 1 iteration(s), 1 application(s)
\end{lstlisting}

The trace is instructive. The graph starts with five classes and five
nodes: the atom \code{a}, the atom \code{0}, the node \code{(add a 0)},
the atom \code{1}, and the node \code{(mul (add a 0) 1)}. The first
application merges the class of \code{(add a 0)} with the class of
\code{a}, which is the \emph{add-zero} law. The second application merges
the class of the multiplication with the same class, which is the
\emph{mul-one} law. The third and fourth applications fire again, this
time against the merged class, and establish nothing new---which is
exactly why the revision counter, not the application counter, decides
that the run has saturated.

The graph ends with three classes and five nodes: the merged class
holding the atom \code{a} together with the two derived list nodes, and
two singleton classes holding the atoms \code{0} and \code{1}. The
extraction picks \code{a}, because an atom costs one node and the list
nodes cost three. Had the rules included a law that made \code{a} itself
expand, the extraction would have chosen the cheaper of the two
alternatives, and it would have done so with the whole class in view.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{No deletion.} A rule never removes an alternative. The
  graph only ever grows, or stays the same. This is the whole point, but
  it means the node budget, not the rule set, is what bounds a run.
\item \textbf{Ground terms only.} \code{add} refuses a term containing a
  pattern variable. There is no way to add a schematic term to the graph
  and match against it.
\item \textbf{Literal operators.} A list whose head is a pattern variable
  is not a valid e-graph term, and a list whose head is itself a list is
  not either.
\item \textbf{Exact arity.} A list pattern matches an e-node only when
  the arities agree. There are no variadic operators and no implicit
  padding.
\item \textbf{Node budget.} The budget is installed only for the duration
  of a saturation. A graph that is grown by \code{add} calls outside a
  saturation is unbounded.
\item \textbf{No cost model.} \code{extract} minimises node count and
  nothing else. There is no user-supplied weight for operators, no
  preference for one alternative over another, and no way to extract a
  term that is not the cheapest one.
\item \textbf{Iteration semantics.} A rule applies at most once per
  matched class per pass, but a class that changes during a pass may be
  matched again by a later rule in the same pass. The pass boundary is
  the only synchronisation point.
\item \textbf{One extraction per class.} \code{extract} returns a single
  representative. Asking for several representatives of the same class
  means asking several times, and each call returns the same answer.
\item \textbf{Match cost.} \code{matches} is a full scan of the root
  classes for every call, and \code{saturate} calls it once per rule per
  pass. The cost of a saturation is therefore roughly the number of passes
  times the number of rules times the size of the graph.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

The e-graph is the mechanism that makes a rewrite system able to answer
``are these two things the same?'' rather than only ``what does this
thing rewrite to?''. Its three moving parts---hash-consed e-nodes,
union-find classes, and a congruence rebuild---are individually simple,
and their interaction is what makes the whole thing work. The next two
